    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The diffusivity equation was derived with the modification of taking the change in porosity of the system into consideration during either depletion or fluid injection. In one dimension the diffusivity equation for isothermal flow then was written as...with the assumption that cp=constant. This can be shown to be very nearly true for most liquids especially at pressures slightly higher than their bubble points. In the above equation it was considered that permeability, k, and porosity, f, are functions of external and internal pressure, while viscosity, compressibility factor, c, and density, of the liquid are only functions of internal pressure.Since D(P) =k/fuc is a function of pressure, the term diffusivity function was adopted to distinguish it from the diffusivity coefficient being used when D(P) = constant.From a survey of the literature it was concluded that D(P) usually has a non-linear relationship with P. Therefore, it was assumed that diffusivity function can be formulized by a quadratic equation of the form S(P) = A + bP + cP2, where a, b, and c are any arbitrary constants. Thus, the diffusivity equation, after several transformations, was written in a dimensionless form as:The above non-linear partial differential equation was solved for four different boundary conditions which are usually encountered in reservoir engineering as well as in hear conduction, diffusion, etc. The four boundary conditions are: Case I Constant terminal pressure for medium or semi-infinite, Case II Constant terminal pressure for medium of finite length, Case III Constant terminal rate for medium of semi-infinite length, Case IV Constant terminal rate for medium of finite length. The series was used as the solution of the above partial differential equation. The general integral equation expressing W(X,T) was written for each case and several terms of each series was calculated analytically or numerically. In addition, the existence and the uniqueness of the solution was proven. The functional relationship of D(P)=k/fuc was studied for Case II with a long sandstone core and a particular liquid. The study of pressure decline curves from different positions of the core show that there is a better correlation between the experimental and theoretical curves than between the experimental curve and the one calculate by assuming D(P) is constant.",
            "type": "abstract",
